---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}
const props = Astro.props;
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="overview">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      CSS gives you several ways to express a length, and the most common four are px, rem, em, and
      pt. They all describe size, but they anchor to different things. Understanding what each one is
      relative to is the difference between a layout that respects a reader's settings and one that
      ignores them. This converter turns any value into all of them at once.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="the-units">
    <h2>What each unit is relative to</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>px</strong> is an absolute CSS pixel. It never changes, which is exactly why it does not scale with a reader's font preference.</li>
      <li><strong>rem</strong> is relative to the root font size set on the html element. One rem is 16px by default, so rem values scale when the root scales.</li>
      <li><strong>em</strong> is relative to the font size of the current element, so nested ems can multiply together.</li>
      <li><strong>pt</strong> is a print point. On the web 1 inch is 96px and 72pt, so 1px is 0.75pt.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to">
    <h2>Converting a value</h2>
    <p>
      Enter a number and choose the unit you are starting from. Set the root font size if your CSS
      changes it from the 16px default. The tool shows the equivalent px, rem, em, pt, and percent
      values, and you can copy any single result. Because everything is anchored to the root size you
      set, the numbers match what your stylesheet will actually render.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="why-rem">
    <h2>Why designers move to rem</h2>
    <p>
      When a reader bumps up their default font size for comfort, a layout built in rem grows with
      them: text, padding, and gaps all scale from the same root. A layout hard-coded in px stays
      fixed and can become cramped or unreadable. Sizing type and spacing in rem is a small change
      that makes an interface far more accessible, which is why most modern design systems prefer it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="root-trick">
    <h2>The 62.5 percent root trick</h2>
    <p>
      Some codebases set the html font size to 62.5 percent, which makes the root 10px so that 1rem
      equals 10px and the math is easy: 2.4rem is 24px. If your project does this, set the root field
      to 10 here and the conversions will line up with your stylesheet. Otherwise leave it at 16.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy">
    <h2>Local and offline</h2>
    <p>
      The conversion is plain arithmetic that runs in your browser. Nothing is sent anywhere, and the
      tool works with no connection once loaded, so it is safe to use on any project.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
